{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>For all the signs and wonders.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): he performed signs and wonders before the eyes of the dissenters until they conceded against their will, and he did so before the eyes of all Israel, who believed in him. From this the prophecy of Moses became renowned throughout the world more than that of all the other prophets, for the prophecy of the other prophets was not known to all but required witnesses, in the manner of what is written (II Kings 8:4): ״Tell me, please, all the great things that Elisha did,״ and it is written (ibid.): ״And the woman said, this is the woman and this is her son, whom Elisha revived.״ But the prophecy of Moses was known and publicized to all the nations, because his signs and wonders were revealed and publicized before the eyes of the dissenters — this is what it says: לפרעה ולכל עבדיו ולכל ארצו — and before the eyes of the believers — this is what it says: לעיני כל ישראל." "<b>And by way of</b> the Midrash: לכל האותות והמופתים — these are the ten plagues in Egypt; and concerning this it says לפרעה ולכל עבדיו ולכל ארצו."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 34, :notes {0 "Source text reads מפוסרמים (in comment 0), apparently a variant spelling of מפורסמים; translated as 'publicized' in keeping with the parallel usage."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:54:43.748583223Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}